From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sun, 9 Aug 2026 00:19:05 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/3] cuda/hip: the GDN gated norm as one kernel, with an
 association fence (+1.0% decode)

The gated-delta-net tail is four graph nodes that stock runs as two launches:

    RMS_NORM -> MUL(ssm_norm.weight)   -> rms_norm_f32<256,true,false>
    UNARY(SILU)(z) -> MUL              -> unary_gated_op_quant_kernel<silu>

The second reads nothing but the first's output and z, and the first already
owns one whole row per block, so the gate, the product and the folded q8_1 store
are an epilogue the norm kernel can run from registers. 30 launches per decode
token.

The interesting part is why the obvious version of this is WRONG. ggml's HIP
backend is compiled with -funsafe-math-optimizations, so the compiler may
REASSOCIATE a product. In the two separate launches the normalised value is
stored to memory and reloaded, and a load is an opaque value that the gate
multiply cannot be reassociated into. Fusing the two expressions into one
removes that barrier, the merged product reassociates, and the result is a real
arithmetic change - measured at 0.59% of perplexity (3.9382 -> 3.9148), six
times the correctness gate, from a fusion that is algebraically an identity.

An arithmetic fence on the normalised value restores exactly the stock
expression structure: an isolated `scale * x * w` tree, then `op(gate) *
<opaque>`. With it the decode-path perplexity is 3.9382 on both arms - bit
identical. This is the general rule for this series: a CO-LAUNCH is
bit-identical by construction because each guest keeps its own statements, but a
producer-consumer FUSION is not, and every value stock materialises through
memory needs a fence where it is kept in a register instead.

The q8_1 store is glu_quant_store, factored out of glu_quant_body and shared
verbatim with the standalone kernel; each warp covers 32 consecutive, 32-aligned
flat destination elements exactly as in the standalone launch. The fusion also
requires the destination to be range-disjoint from both inputs: ggml-alloc frees
the GDN output as soon as the RMS_NORM is processed, and stock is only safe over
an overlap because the two kernels are ordered.

GGML_CUDA_RMS_NORM_GATE_FUSE=0 restores the two separate launches.
